Q: Iterate through multiple items and remove the first child of every item using jQuery A: <p>Here are some examples in <strong>pure JavaScript</strong> (no jQuery needed) that achieve the same result.<br />
It's all in using the proper selector:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>//Use either one of the following


// Handle exclusively .image_item but ignore other selectors
// (keeps other unrelated elements)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image :nth-child(n+2 of .image_item)&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// Handle exclusively .image_item but ignore other selectors
// Removes any .image_item that is a subsequent of .image_item
// (keeps other unrelated elements)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image .image_item ~ .image_item&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// Use this if the child selector does not matters
// (since given your markup you only have one class of children)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image :nth-child(n+2)&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// (will not work if you have another element as first child)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image .image_item:nth-child(n+2)&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// (will not work if you have another element as first child)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image .image_item:not(:first-child)&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// (will not work if you have another element as first child)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image .image_item:not(:nth-child(1))&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// Remove any .image_item that immediately follows a .image_item
// (will not work if you have any other element after any .image_item)
document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.image .image_item + .image_item&quot;).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());
</code></pre>
<p>all of the above work directly with your specific HTML markup, but make sure to use the one that fits best your needs given the comments in code.</p>
<hr />
<p>If you want to create a useful utility function <code>remove(selector, ElementParent?)</code></p>
<pre><code>const remove = (sel, par = document) =&gt; par.querySelectorAll(sel).forEach(el =&gt; el.remove());

// Use either one of the following:
remove(&quot;.image :nth-child(n+2 of .image_item)&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image .image_item ~ .image_item&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image :nth-child(n+2)&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image .image_item:nth-child(n+2)&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image .image_item:not(:first-child)&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image .image_item:not(:nth-child(1))&quot;);
remove(&quot;.image .image_item + .image_item&quot;);
</code></pre>

Q: How to globally import some module in Angular with Standalone Components? A: <p>Not currently possible as far as I'm aware. The standard approach to this is to use a shared module for the code. Here's an example:</p>
<pre><code>import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import { FormsModule, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

// Example: Angular Material modules
import { MatButtonModule } from '@angular/material/button';
import { MatInputModule } from '@angular/material/input';

// Example: Custom pipes/directives
import { MyPipe } from './pipes/my.pipe';
import { MyDirective } from './directives/my.directive';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    MyPipe,
    MyDirective
  ],
  imports: [
    CommonModule,
    FormsModule,
    ReactiveFormsModule,
    MatButtonModule,
    MatInputModule
  ],
  exports: [
    CommonModule,
    FormsModule,
    ReactiveFormsModule,
    MatButtonModule,
    MatInputModule,
    MyPipe,
    MyDirective
  ]
})
export class SharedModule {}
</code></pre>
